   "evidence": "grep: `DeRham.lean:78: private def wedgeLeftL (k : \u2115) : E \u2192L[\u211d] (\u22c0[\u211d]^k E \u2192L[\u211d] \u22c0[\u211d]^(k + 1) E)` is used in both public definitions (`DeRham.lean:98` and `:180`). No other bundled version of `wedgeLeft` in the vector variable exists in TauCeti (only `def wedgeLeft (m : M) (n : \u2115)` at ExteriorPower/Basic.lean:521).",
   "file": "TauCeti/Analysis/Pseudodifferential/DeRham.lean",
   "fix": "Make it public in its canonical home: put a linear `ExteriorAlgebra.wedgeLeft\u2097 : M \u2192\u2097[R] \u22c0[R]^n M \u2192\u2097[R] \u22c0[R]^(n+1) M` in LinearAlgebra/ExteriorPower/Basic.lean, stating `wedgeLeft_add`/`wedgeLeft_smul` as its `map_add`/`map_smul`, and put the continuous version with a `@[simp]` `_apply` lemma in HodgeStar/Contraction.lean, which already carries the normed structure and is imported by DeRham.lean.",
   "issue": "`private def wedgeLeftL` and `private theorem wedgeLeftL_apply` bundle `ExteriorAlgebra.wedgeLeft` as a continuous linear function of the vector. The statement involves only `\u22c0[\u211d]^k E` and the proof uses only `wedgeLeft_add`/`wedgeLeft_smul`, nothing about de Rham symbols. Hiding it means downstream users, for example of the Dirac or Clifford case advertised in FirstOrder.lean or anyone writing `ofRealLinear X (\u03be \u21a6 \u03be \u2227 \u00b7)`, cannot name the family that `deRhamSymbol` and `extDeriv` are defined from.",
   "line": 78
  }
 ],
